---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../consts';
---

<Base title={`Privacy policy — ${SITE.name}`} description="How CamLock handles face data, permissions and the on-device event log.">
  <section>
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Legal</p>
      <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
      <p class="lede">Last updated {SITE.lastUpdated}.</p>

      <p>
        CamLock is an Android app that recognises the children a parent enrols and locks the phone
        when one of them is seen. It has no server component, no account system and no analytics.
        This policy describes every piece of data the app handles.
      </p>

      <h2>Data CamLock collects</h2>
      <p>
        CamLock collects nothing about you and transmits nothing off the device. The data below is
        created on your phone, stays on your phone, and is removed when you delete it or uninstall
        the app.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Face templates.</strong> When you enrol a child, the front camera or the photos
          you pick are converted into numeric face templates. The photos and camera frames
          themselves are not kept. Templates are encrypted with a key held in the Android Keystore
          and cannot be converted back into an image.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Child profiles.</strong> The name you type, the enrolment date, and the per-child
          options you set, such as a custom lock message or tracking-only mode.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Event log.</strong> Timestamped records of checks, locks, unlocks, protection
          starting and stopping, calls allowed from the lock screen, tamper detections and
          enrolment changes. Retention is configurable and defaults to 90 days, after which old
          events are purged automatically.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Settings.</strong> Your configuration of the app.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Camera use</h2>
      <p>
        The front camera opens for roughly 2.5 seconds per check, triggered when protection starts,
        when the screen turns on, after an unlock, when a different app comes to the front, and on
        a follow-up shortly after each of those. Frames are processed in memory for face detection
        and matching, then discarded. No image or video is written to storage or sent anywhere.
        Android shows its camera privacy indicator on every check.
      </p>

      <h2>Permissions and why they are needed</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Camera</strong> — to run the face checks.</li>
        <li><strong>Usage Access</strong> — to notice when a different app comes to the front, which is both a check trigger and how the lock screen is restored over apps that hide overlays.</li>
        <li><strong>Display over other apps</strong> — to show the lock screen.</li>
        <li><strong>Notifications</strong> — for the permanent "protection is running" notification, the reboot reminder and tamper alerts.</li>
        <li><strong>Phone state</strong> — to stand aside while a call is ringing or active, so calls are never blocked.</li>
        <li><strong>Battery</strong> (optional, not a permission) — CamLock only opens Android's battery optimisation list, where you can turn optimisation off for CamLock so the system is less likely to stop protection in the background.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Sharing</h2>
      <p>
        CamLock does not share, sell or transfer any data. There are no third-party SDKs for
        advertising, analytics or crash reporting, and the app makes no network requests of its
        own. Face recognition uses a model bundled inside the app and runs locally.
      </p>

      <h2>Children</h2>
      <p>
        CamLock is used by a parent or guardian on their own phone and is not directed at children.
        A child never uses the app; they only see the lock screen. The face data of an enrolled
        child is created by the parent, stored encrypted on that parent's device, and removed when
        the parent removes the child or deletes all data.
      </p>

      <h2>Your control over the data</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Remove a single child, which deletes their templates and profile.</li>
        <li>Reset a child's face samples without deleting the profile.</li>
        <li>Export the event log as a CSV file through the system file picker.</li>
        <li>Delete all data in one action in Settings: children, templates, events and settings.</li>
        <li>Uninstall the app, which removes all of it.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Security</h2>
      <p>
        Face templates are encrypted with AES-GCM using a key generated in and held by the Android
        Keystore. Opening CamLock, or returning to it from the background, requires a strong
        biometric; the device PIN is not accepted as a fallback. The same applies to unlocking a
        locked phone.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If this policy changes, the updated version is published on this page with a new date.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about this policy: <a href={`mailto:${SITE.supportEmail}`}>{SITE.supportEmail}</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose { max-width: 44rem; }
  .prose h1 { font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 5vw, 3.2rem); letter-spacing: -0.04em; line-height: 1.05; }
  .prose h2 { font-size: 1.35rem; margin-top: 2.25rem; }
  .prose ul { padding-left: 1.2rem; margin: 0 0 1em; }
  .prose li { margin-bottom: 0.6rem; color: var(--muted); }
  .prose li strong { color: var(--text); }
  .prose p { color: var(--muted); }
  .prose .lede { color: var(--muted); font-size: 1rem; }
</style>
